# Copy to `.env.edge` and fill in:  cp .env.edge.example .env.edge
#
# The edge secrets, kept OUT of `.env` on purpose. docker-compose.yml hands `.env` to the
# `project` (app) container wholesale with `env_file`, so anything written there is readable by
# the packaged UE app; these two have no reader inside it and belong to the login proxy only.
#
# Read by the `oidc-proxy` service and by nothing else. Its env_file entry is `required: false`,
# so a checkout without this file still renders with `docker compose config` -- you need it to
# run the OIDC login gate, not to bring the rest of the stack up.
#
# Two rules for anything you add here:
#
#  * Spell keys with the name the CONSUMING PROCESS reads, as the two below use oauth2-proxy's own
#    OAUTH2_PROXY_* names rather than the CI_PORTAL_* / OIDC_COOKIE_SECRET names .env.example gave
#    them. env_file injects a file's keys into the container under those keys and cannot rename on
#    the way, and re-exporting them through a compose `environment:` entry does not work either:
#    such an entry renders as "" when its variable is unset, and an empty `environment:` value
#    overrides env_file.
#  * Nothing here can be read by `${...}` anywhere in a compose file. env_file feeds a container's
#    environment only, never compose interpolation. TURN_PASSWORD is in `.env` and not here for
#    exactly that reason -- see the note above it there.
#
# Gitignoring: the repo-root .gitignore lists `Deployment/.env`, and `Deployment/.env.edge` needs
# its own line beside it. Check `git status` before you commit until that line exists; a filled-in
# copy of this file is a live client secret.

# OIDC client secret, issued by ci-portal when you register this deployment as a client.
OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me-oidc-client-secret

# 32-byte base64/hex random string: `openssl rand -base64 32`
OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET=change-me-32-byte-random
